<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/reading-room/">Reading room</a><span>/</span>Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Lawyer: Choosing the Right Help</nav><span class="kicker light fade-up">Guide · 6 min read · October 15, 2026</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Lawyer: Choosing the Right Help</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Appraisers, public adjusters and attorneys solve different problems. Matching the help to the actual dispute saves time and money.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Start by naming the problem</h2>
<p>When a property claim goes badly, the instinct is to hire someone fast. The better first step is to name what has actually gone wrong. Is the carrier refusing to pay at all? Paying, but too little? Or is the claim simply too big and complicated to manage alone? Each of those problems points to a different kind of help.</p>
<h2>What an appraiser does</h2>
<p>An appraiser in this sense is the person you name under your policy’s appraisal clause. The job is valuation: inspecting, measuring, pricing and negotiating the amount of loss with the carrier’s appraiser, with an umpire breaking ties.</p>
<h3>Best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li>The carrier accepts the claim but its estimate is well below yours.</li>
<li>The dispute is about scope, measurements, pricing or repair method.</li>
<li>You want a binding number without a lawsuit.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<p>Appraisal decides amount of loss, not coverage. An appraiser doesn’t argue policy interpretation, handle the whole claim from first notice, or give legal advice.</p>
<h2>What a public adjuster does</h2>
<p>A public adjuster is licensed to represent policyholders in presenting and negotiating a claim. Public adjusters generally take over claim management: documenting the loss, preparing the estimate and contents inventory, and dealing with the adjuster. Many are paid a percentage of what the claim pays, so ask how the fee applies to money already offered.</p>
<h3>Best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li>A large or complex loss, such as a wildfire total loss with a long contents list.</li>
<li>You don’t have the time or energy to manage the claim yourself.</li>
<li>The claim is early and hasn’t yet hardened into a specific dispute.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<p>A public adjuster can’t give legal advice or file suit. Check that anyone you hire is licensed through the New Mexico Office of Superintendent of Insurance.</p>
<h2>What an attorney does</h2>
<p>An attorney advises you on your legal rights and can sue on your behalf. In New Mexico, the Insurance Code (NMSA 1978, Chapter 59A, Article 16) lists unfair claims practices, and New Mexico courts recognize bad-faith claims against insurers. Whether any of that applies to your situation is a question only a New Mexico attorney can answer.</p>
<h3>Best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li>The claim has been denied, in whole or in large part.</li>
<li>The carrier has issued a reservation of rights or is raising coverage defenses.</li>
<li>You’re worried about a suit limitation date or other legal deadline.</li>
<li>You believe the claim has been handled unfairly or in bad faith.</li>
</ul>
<h2>A quick comparison</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Underpaid but accepted claim:</strong> appraisal is often the most direct route.</li>
<li><strong>Large, messy claim early on:</strong> a public adjuster may help manage it.</li>
<li><strong>Denial, coverage fight or conduct concerns:</strong> talk to a New Mexico attorney.</li>
<li><strong>Not sure:</strong> an <a href="/appraiser-services/claim-estimate-review/">estimate review</a> can show whether the gap is about valuation at all.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">They can work together</b>These roles aren’t mutually exclusive. An attorney may recommend appraisal to resolve the amount while handling legal issues separately, and a public adjuster may invoke appraisal on a claim they manage.</div>
<h2>Questions to ask anyone you hire</h2>
<ol>
<li>What exactly will you do, and what won’t you do?</li>
<li>How are you paid, and is that in writing?</li>
<li>How does your fee treat money the carrier already offered?</li>
<li>Do you have any relationship with my carrier or contractor?</li>
<li>Who will I actually be working with day to day?</li>
</ol>
<h2>Timing matters</h2>
<p>The right help can change as a claim moves along. Early on, while the carrier is still inspecting and writing estimates, careful documentation and a clear supplement request may be all you need. Once the carrier has made its position clear and the dispute is narrowed to a number, appraisal becomes a stronger option. If a denial letter arrives, or a reservation of rights, the legal questions take priority. Bringing in the wrong kind of help at the wrong stage tends to cost time, and sometimes money, without moving the claim forward.</p>
<h2>What to have ready before you call anyone</h2>
<ul>
<li>A complete copy of your policy, including the declarations page and endorsements.</li>
<li>The carrier’s estimate and any payment letters.</li>
<li>Your contractor’s estimate, if you have one.</li>
<li>Photos and video of the damage.</li>
<li>Correspondence with the adjuster, including emails and notes from calls.</li>
</ul>
<p>Having these in one place lets any professional give you a useful answer in the first conversation. Our <a href="/claim-estimators/appraisal-file-checkup/">appraisal file checkup</a> walks through the list.</p>
<h2>Free help that’s easy to overlook</h2>
<p>The Office of Superintendent of Insurance accepts consumer complaints about insurers and claim handling. Filing a complaint doesn’t replace appraisal or legal advice, but it creates a record and sometimes prompts a closer look at a stalled claim.</p>
<h2>Where we fit</h2>
<p>We’re independent appraisers. We don’t manage whole claims, and we don’t give legal advice. If the carrier has accepted your claim and you disagree on the number, see our <a href="/appraiser-services/">appraiser services</a> or read <a href="/frequently-asked/">frequently asked questions</a>. If your problem looks like a job for an attorney or public adjuster, we’ll say so. <a href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">Contact us</a> to talk it through.</p>
